            "text": "In a sorting warehouse outside Seattle, the night shift increasingly consists of two kinds of worker: humans who still outnumber the robots, and robots whose share of the floor grows every quarter without anyone announcing a single mass layoff. It is this quiet, incremental substitution, rather than any dramatic robot uprising, that is actually reshaping the modern labour market. What gets lost in breathless headlines about job-destroying machines is the slower, messier reality: automation rarely eliminates an entire occupation overnight; it chips away at tasks within it until the remaining human role looks unrecognisable. Consider radiology, where software can now flag tumours as accurately as many specialists, yet demand for radiologists has not collapsed, because the job quietly expanded to include tasks the algorithm cannot do, such as communicating difficult diagnoses to frightened patients. It is precisely this pattern, task erosion rather than wholesale disappearance, that economists increasingly point to when asked which jobs are safe. No occupation is entirely safe from this process, yet few vanish completely either; most simply mutate into something narrower and more specialised. What used to be considered secure, decades-long employment with a single firm is increasingly giving way to short, flexible, often precarious arrangements. It is the rise of the gig economy that best captures this shift, as millions of drivers, couriers and freelance coders now piece together an income from several platforms rather than one employer. For some, this arrangement has brought a genuine sense of autonomy: a parent who can choose which hours to work, or a graduate testing several careers before committing to one. For others, it has brought only the chronic anxiety of looming redundancy, with no severance pay and no clear path back into stable work. What companies now demand of their staff, more than any specific technical skill, is the willingness to upskill continuously, since expertise that commands a premium today may be obsolete within five years. A logistics firm in Rotterdam offers an instructive case: it retrained an entire generation of warehouse pickers as drone-fleet supervisors rather than making them redundant, a choice it says cost more in the short term but saved far more in institutional knowledge. Such examples remain the exception rather than the rule, and critics are right to point out that retraining promises are often cheaper to announce than to deliver. What remains genuinely unresolved is whether this constant churn will, on balance, expand individual autonomy or merely redistribute insecurity more widely across the workforce. It is governments, ultimately, that will have to decide how unemployment insurance, pensions and training budgets should be redesigned for workers who may hold a dozen different jobs across a single career. What seems clear, whatever else remains contested, is that the future of work will reward adaptability far more generously than it rewards any single fixed skill."
