            "text": "A software company in Reykjavik made headlines a few years ago, not for a product launch, but for moving nearly its entire workforce to a four-day week without cutting pay, and then publishing the results: productivity held steady, and reported burnout fell sharply. Mental health has moved, in little more than a decade, from the margins of public conversation to the centre of corporate strategy and public policy alike. Awareness campaigns have undoubtedly reduced some of the stigma once attached to conditions such as anxiety and depression; that said, surveys consistently show that many people still hesitate to disclose a diagnosis to an employer for fear of being quietly sidelined. Workplaces, in light of rising rates of burnout documented across most developed economies, have begun introducing wellbeing programmes, flexible hours and designated mental health days. Consequently, some employees report feeling more supported than their predecessors did a generation ago, when such language barely existed in a professional context at all. Critics argue, nevertheless, that such initiatives can amount to little more than a sympathetic gesture if the underlying workload that caused the exhaustion in the first place remains untouched. A free meditation app, however well designed, does not change a culture of unanswerable late-night emails; it merely offers a coping mechanism for surviving one. Resilience training, for instance, is sometimes promoted by employers as a substitute for structural change, quietly shifting the burden of adaptation onto individuals rather than onto the institutions that set the pace of their work. That is not to dismiss resilience as a concept entirely; the capacity to recover from setbacks is a genuinely valuable skill, built partly through practice and partly through circumstance. It is simply to note that resilience training aimed at employees means little if it is never matched by comparable attention to the conditions those employees are expected to be resilient against. Moreover, access to professional support remains deeply unequal even within wealthy countries, with waiting lists of many months in public systems and prohibitive hourly rates in private ones. By contrast, informal coping mechanisms such as regular exercise, sustained social contact and adequate sleep are inexpensive, well evidenced, and routinely underfunded in public health messaging compared with their actual importance. Notwithstanding these gaps, there is encouraging evidence from several national health systems that early intervention, even brief and relatively low-cost, significantly improves long-term outcomes for common anxiety disorders. The Icelandic experiment mentioned earlier is instructive precisely because it addressed structure rather than symptoms: fewer hours, not more mindfulness apps, produced the measurable improvement. In light of all this, policymakers and employers alike face a genuine choice between treating wellbeing as an individual responsibility to be managed with better coping strategies, or as a collective one to be addressed by redesigning how people are actually asked to work. The evidence, on balance, points firmly toward the second answer, even though the first remains considerably cheaper to announce."
